surviving.proposed_fix:
Move the watchdog body into a repo module, ops/watchdog.py, so it is versioned, tested and promoted. launch_job.run_watchdog becomes a one-line call. It must report:
- every scoped halt, via halt.other_book_halts(None), with its age;
- for the cef family, consecutive trading days with no armed beat, judged from the verifier's 'decision' check or from beat status. Any day the book was expected to trade and did not is reported;
- the last verify_<book> beat when it is failed or unverified, or when email_delivered is false;
- staleness for cef_pm and verify_* as well.
Add watchdog fires at about 09:15 and 12:30 on trading days (a plist change). Invariant: the watchdog status may be 'ok' only if no HALT*.md exists and the last expected decision armed or stood down for a named, alerted reason.

Keep launch_job.py thin and out-of-repo as it is; do not move it. Add a repo helper, ops/session_report.py::finish(job, refresh_rc, capture_rc, ...), that launch_job calls. It:
- adds 'refresh failed rc=N: <INCOMPLETE line>' and 'capture failed rc=N' to the beat's blockers and to the dry-run reason;
- alerts on either;
- for capture, retries once after 60 s before alerting.
Route the launch_job.py edit through the existing patch tool pattern (ops/schedule/patch_launch_job_w3am.py), so the change is reviewed and tested even though the file is unversioned.

Cheapest real protection, in two parts.
(A) ops/panel_guard.py::validate(asof, universe) -> list of violations. Checks:
- every deployed name is present in both panels;
- (date, ticker) is unique;
- no weekend dates;
- each deployed name has at least z_window+80 paired rows, with z_window read from the frozen spec, not a literal;
- asof values are finite and >0;
- the row count is not lower than the manifest's;
- historical closes older than 5 sessions are unchanged against the manifest hash, per name.
fetch_daily calls it BEFORE atomic_write and refuses to write on a violation, then writes data/cef/_panel_manifest.json (the sha256 of each parquet, rows, per-name last date, writer, pid, timestamp). preflight.check_data calls it again and blocks when the on-disk sha no longer matches the manifest: 'panel changed since the refresh that validated it'.
(B) stage_cef.py and fetch_cef_distributions.py write to data/cef/_staging/ by default. They overwrite a live panel only with --replace-live, and refuse that flag while any com.quantt.cef* job has a pid or when the new frame lacks a deployed name.
The structural fix, a prod-owned data/cef with the symlink removed, stays a human decision; sync_dev_data.sh already assumes it.

